= Version control =

== Overview ==
'''Version control''' is a system for recording changes to files or sets of files over time so earlier states can be inspected or restored.

== Definition ==
A version-control system tracks revisions rather than treating the latest file as the only state that matters. Systems may be centralized or distributed; Git is a widely used distributed version-control system.

== Scope & importance ==
Version control preserves change history. It enables comparison, attribution, branching, restoration, collaboration, review, and reproducible development workflows.

== Core concepts ==
Common concepts include repository, commit, history, diff, branch, merge, tag, remote, working tree, and conflict resolution.

== Examples ==
A developer can compare two commits to identify when a behavior changed. A documentation team can work on a branch and review the diff before merging it into the canonical branch.

== Applications / implications ==
Version control is used for software, documentation, research artifacts, configuration, websites, structured text, and other files whose history matters.

== Epistemic notes ==
A version-control history records changes, not truth. A newer revision may still be wrong; review and source validation remain separate responsibilities.

